  "response_text": "Yes, the OS will still work with only a single thread. After the thread runs and calls yield, the scheduler (osSched) is triggered and attempts to select the next thread. Since there's only one thread in the system, it is still marked as ready and is the only candidate to run, so the scheduler selects it again. The OS performs a context switch back to the same thread, effectively wasting a few cycles, but continuing to function correctly. The thread repeatedly runs, yields, and is rescheduled, creating a cycle that keeps the system alive without issue.\\n",
